The closed-loop bandwidth of the amplifier is \[f_{BW}=\frac{\text{Gain-Bandwidth Product}}{\text{Gain}}
=\frac{1\,\text{MHz}}{10}
=100\,\text{kHz}.\] Since the input frequency is only \(10\,\text{kHz}\), the amplifier bandwidth is sufficient to reproduce the waveform. Hence, the output is limited only by the slew rate. The maximum output swing due to the slew rate is \[V_{o(p)}=\frac{\text{SR}\times T}{4},\] where \[\text{SR}=0.1\,\text{V}/\mu\text{s}, \qquad
T=\frac{1}{10\,\text{kHz}}=100\,\mu\text{s}.\] Therefore, \[V_{o(p)}
=\frac{0.1\times100}{4}
=2.5\,\text{V}.\] Since the required output amplitude (\(10\times10=100\,\text{V}\)) is much larger than the slew-rate-limited value, the output becomes a triangular wave. \[\boxed{\text{(d) }\pm2.5\,\text{V}\text{ amplitude triangular wave}}\]

